---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout title="Small batch sticker printing from 50 units" description="Small batch custom stickers from 50 units: die cut or sheets, white or clear vinyl, priced for a first run rather than for a container load.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Small batch sticker printing" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Small batch sticker printing</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.08rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        Fifty stickers is a real order, not a sample request. It covers a launch, a market stall, a
        subscriber insert or a first packaging run — without a box of 5,000 in the spare room.
      </p>

      <h2>What small batch is for</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Launching a brand</th><td>Logo stickers and packaging seals before volume is known</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Testing a design</th><td>See how a shape and finish look on the actual packaging</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Events and markets</th><td>Giveaways and price tags for a single weekend</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Ecommerce inserts</th><td>Sticker sheets in the first hundred orders</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Choosing the format at low volume</h2>
      <p>
        Two formats suit a first run. Individual die cut stickers are the simplest: one design, one shape, peel and
        apply. Sticker sheets put several designs on one backing sheet — better when a customer should receive a set,
        or when the stickers are too small to handle individually.
      </p>
      <p>
        At 50 units, a simple shape avoids a custom die and keeps the price down. Complex contours need a cutting
        path and, above a few hundred units, a steel rule die — which is a one-off cost that rarely makes sense
        at this quantity.
      </p>

      <h2>Material at small volume</h2>
      <p>
        White vinyl is the default: water-resistant, durable and available in the thickness that feeds through most
        printers. Clear vinyl gives the no-label look but needs white ink behind the design, which adds a pass.
        Paper is cheaper and fine for indoor, short-life use.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lamination is the durability decision. On 50 stickers it is a small absolute cost and a large difference
        in how the sticker survives a bag, a water bottle or a shop window.
      </p>

      <h2>What changes when you scale</h2>
      <p>
        Price per sticker falls sharply between 50 and 500 because setup is amortised; after that it declines more
        slowly. The other change is format: above a few thousand, rolls and steel-rule dies become the sensible
        production method, and the artwork needs to be set up for them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reordering is easier than ordering: the approved proof and specifications carry over, so the second run
        skips the artwork review and moves straight to production.
      </p>

      <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/request-a-quote/">Request a quote for 50+ stickers</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand subject="Small batch sticker quotation" body={`Tell us the size, quantity, material and destination. Minimum ${site.moq} units.`} />
</BaseLayout>
